      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-title">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="currentColor"><path d="M9 16.17L4.83 12l-1.42 1.41L9 19 21 7l-1.41-1.41z"/></svg>
          Executive Summary: 2026 Medicare Changes
        </div>
        <ul class="callout-list">
          <li><strong>The $2,000 Part D Cap:</strong> Under the Inflation Reduction Act, annual out-of-pocket spending on covered Part D prescriptions tops out at <strong>$2,000</strong>. The old "doughnut hole" coverage gap is gone.</li>
          <li><strong>Key Dates:</strong> Medicare Fall Open Enrollment runs from <strong>October 15 through December 7</strong> every year, with any new plan starting January 1.</li>
          <li><strong>Watch for Quiet Plan Changes:</strong> Because insurers now pick up more catastrophic drug bills under the $2,000 cap, many have shifted drugs into pricier tiers, added prior authorizations, or trimmed dental and vision perks. Reading your Annual Notice of Change (ANOC) pays off.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p class="lead-text">
        Too many people treat Medicare like a set-it-and-forget-it utility bill. Every fall, insurers quietly redraw doctor networks and reshuffle drug lists, and the 65 million Americans on Medicare get one clean window to respond before January 1. Skip that review and you could walk into a pharmacy in January to find your regular prescription bumped from Tier 2 to Tier 4.
      </p>

      <h2>The Medicare Enrollment Calendar: Three Distinct Periods</h2>
      <p>
        Medicare's overlapping windows trip up almost everyone at first. Here is what each period actually lets you do:
      </p>

      <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Enrollment Period</th>
              <th>Applicable Dates</th>
              <th>Permitted Actions</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Annual Open Enrollment Period (AEP)</strong></td>
              <td>October 15 – December 7</td>
              <td>Switch from Original Medicare to Advantage, switch between Advantage plans, switch Part D drug plans, or return to Original Medicare.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Medicare Advantage Open Enrollment (OEP)</strong></td>
              <td>January 1 – March 31</td>
              <td>For individuals <em>already enrolled</em> in Medicare Advantage: Switch to another Advantage plan or return to Original Medicare with a Part D drug plan.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Initial Enrollment Period (IEP)</strong></td>
              <td>7-month window around 65th birthday</td>
              <td>Enroll in Medicare Part A and Part B for the first time without incurring lifelong late enrollment penalties.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>How the $2,000 Prescription Drug Cap Transforms 2026 Coverage</h2>
      <p>
        Capping Part D drug spending at $2,000 a year is the biggest break Medicare enrollees have seen since the drug benefit launched in 2006. Before this rule took hold, patients taking specialty biologics for cancer, rheumatoid arthritis, or macular degeneration routinely paid $8,000 to $12,000 a year out of pocket.
      </p>

      <div class="card" style="margin: 1.5rem 0;">
        <h4 style="color: var(--color-primary); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">The Medicare Prescription Payment Plan (M3P) Option:</h4>
        <p>
          Alongside the $2,000 cap, you can opt into the <strong>Medicare Prescription Payment Plan</strong>. Instead of getting hit with an $800 pharmacy bill in January or February, you spread your drug costs into steady, interest-free monthly payments across the year.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>The Top 4 Mistakes Beneficiaries Make During Open Enrollment</h2>

      <h3>1. Ignoring the Annual Notice of Change (ANOC) Letter</h3>
      <p>
        Every September, your insurer mails out an <em>Annual Notice of Change</em>. Plenty of people toss it straight into the recycling bin with the junk mail. Don't. That packet spells out every change hitting your plan on January 1, including higher copays, dropped doctors, hospital exits, and drug-tier changes.
      </p>

      <h3>2. Choosing a Plan Based Solely on a $0 Monthly Premium</h3>
      <p>
        A $0-premium Medicare Advantage plan is not always the cheapest option over 12 months. It can easily cost more than a $35-a-month plan if it charges a $450 daily inpatient hospital copay for the first five days or bills 20% coinsurance on outpatient chemotherapy.
      </p>

      <h3>3. Assuming Your Doctors Will Remain in Network</h3>
      <p>
        Contract fights between major hospital systems and private Medicare Advantage insurers like UnitedHealthcare and Humana have gotten sharper. Online directories lag behind those breakups, so call your doctor's billing office directly and ask whether they will still take your exact plan next year.
      </p>

      <h3>4. Forgetting That Medigap Does Not Follow Open Enrollment Rules</h3>
      <p>
        Fall Open Enrollment (Oct 15 – Dec 7) lets you switch Medicare Advantage or Part D plans freely. What it <strong>does not</strong> give you is guaranteed approval for a Medigap policy. If you try to leave Medicare Advantage for a Medigap Plan G later on, insurers in most states can check your medical history and deny coverage for pre-existing conditions.
      </p>

      <h2>Worked Drug-Cost Comparison: Two Plans Under the $2,000 Cap</h2>
      <p>
        Even with the $2,000 ceiling in place, the wrong Part D plan can still cost you hundreds extra over a year. Picture someone taking one Tier 2 generic blood-pressure drug and one Tier 3 brand-name blood thinner:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Plan A ($18/month premium, $590 deductible):</strong> Yearly premiums come to $216. With the brand-name drug on preferred Tier 3 ($47/month copay after the deductible), total yearly spending on premiums plus prescriptions lands around <strong>$1,140</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Plan B ($0/month premium, $590 deductible, brand drug moved to non-preferred Tier 4):</strong> The $0 headline price looks tempting, but 35% coinsurance on that brand-name drug pushes out-of-pocket costs straight to the full <strong>$2,000 cap</strong>. That works out to <strong>$860 more</strong> than Plan A for the exact same pills.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Step-by-Step ANOC Review Checklist (Takes 20 Minutes)</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Open the official <a href="https://www.medicare.gov/plan-compare/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Medicare.gov Plan Finder</a> and type in your exact prescriptions, dosages, and preferred pharmacy.</li>
        <li>Confirm that every specialist you saw over the past 12 months will still be in your Medicare Advantage network on January 1.</li>
        <li>Look past the monthly premium to the Maximum Out-of-Pocket (MOOP) limit, and check prior-authorization rules for scans or outpatient surgery.</li>
        <li>If you are in your first 12 months on a Medicare Advantage plan, check your federal <strong>Medigap Trial Right</strong> before that 12-month window closes.</li>
      </ol>

